    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_what",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "What a monohybrid cross is",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Monohybrid",
        "margin_note": "Definition and the actual cross Mendel made. Five lines.",
        "lines": [
          "A MONOHYBRID CROSS is a cross between two",
          "individuals differing in ONE character.",
          "Mendel crossed a pure TALL pea plant (TT) with",
          "a pure DWARF pea plant (tt) to study the",
          "inheritance of one gene."
        ],
        "why": "Following one character at a time is what made the pattern visible; with several characters at once the ratios would have been hidden in each other.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying monohybrid means one parent. It means ONE CHARACTER.",
          "Using impure parents. Both parents must be PURE (homozygous)."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Mono means one: one character, followed alone."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_cross",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "The cross itself",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "The cross",
        "margin_note": "Write both generations with both ratios. Keep genotype and phenotype separate.",
        "lines": [
          "P generation:  Tall (TT)  ×  Dwarf (tt)",
          "Gametes:  T  and  t",
          "F₁ generation:  ALL Tt, and all TALL.",
          "The dwarf character has disappeared.",
          "F₁ selfed:  Tt × Tt",
          "F₂ generation:  1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt",
          "which is 3 TALL : 1 DWARF."
        ],
        "why": "The dwarf character vanishing in F1 and returning in exactly a quarter of F2 is the observation the law was written to explain.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing the F1 as a mixture of tall and dwarf. ALL of F1 is tall.",
          "Giving the F2 phenotypic ratio as 1:2:1. That is the GENOTYPIC ratio; the phenotypic ratio is 3:1."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "All tall in F1, three to one in F2."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_law",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "The law of dominance",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "The law",
        "margin_note": "Number the statements exactly as the law is written.",
        "lines": [
          "The LAW OF DOMINANCE states:",
          "1. Characters are controlled by discrete units",
          "called FACTORS (now called genes).",
          "2. Factors occur in PAIRS.",
          "3. In a dissimilar pair of factors, ONE member",
          "DOMINATES the other."
        ],
        "why": "The three statements together explain why one parent's character can disappear entirely in F1 without being destroyed.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Giving fewer than three statements. Each one is part of the mark.",
          "Saying the recessive factor is destroyed. It is only DOMINATED, and it reappears in F2."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Discrete, paired, one dominating: three statements."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_explains",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "What the law explains",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Explains",
        "margin_note": "Close on what is explained, with the ratio named.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "One character in F₁, both in F₂, in 3 : 1",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "4. The law explains why only ONE parental",
          "character appears in the F₁ generation, and why",
          "BOTH appear in the F₂ generation.",
          "It also explains the 3 : 1 phenotypic ratio",
          "obtained in F₂."
        ],
        "why": "A law in science is judged by what it explains, so the answer has to end with the two observations this one accounts for.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Ending at the three statements. The fourth point is what the law EXPLAINS.",
          "Leaving out the 3:1 ratio. It is the number the law was written to account for."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The law is only useful because it explains the three to one."
